.claude/plans/PLAN-161/pair-rail/round-1.md:13:6. **P1 — C3’s literal `timeout` design is not portable to the repository’s macOS path.** The plan requires `timeout ${N}` (`.claude/plans/PLAN-161-maintenance-sweep.md:267-271`), while the repository’s installer already documents and handles platforms lacking GNU `timeout`, probing `timeout`, then `gtimeout`, then a bounded Python implementation (`scripts/install.sh:1099-1107`, `scripts/install.sh:1125-1135`). On the current macOS reviewer environment, neither `timeout` nor `gtimeout` is installed. **Change:** specify a portable process-group watchdog with the same fallback sequence and test the no-GNU-timeout branch. Also compute scope from actual bounded files/bytes rather than the brief string: the brief only contains the supplied scope text (`.claude/workflows/council-audit.js:126-140`), so `"."` is a one-character string representing the entire repository.
.claude/plans/PLAN-161/pair-rail/round-1.md:15:7. **P1 — C4’s acceptance criteria contradict its fail-fast design, and its timeout proof is incomplete.** The design intentionally fails when a pre-probe remains contended (`.claude/plans/PLAN-161-maintenance-sweep.md:272-279`), yet closeout says any load-window failure requiring a manual rerun is unacceptable (`.claude/plans/PLAN-161-maintenance-sweep.md:343-347`) and success requires surviving load without a rerun (`.claude/plans/PLAN-161-maintenance-sweep.md:427-429`). The cited estimate says `2×420 ≈940` (`.claude/plans/PLAN-161-maintenance-sweep.md:72-75`), although the capped attempts total 840 seconds; the additional backoff and probe budget is unspecified. The real workflow has a 16-minute job limit (`.github/workflows/validate.yml:1181-1183`), two 420-second attempts (`.github/workflows/validate.yml:1222-1231`), and separate control/floor steps (`.github/workflows/validate.yml:1272-1305`). The proposed 25-minute budget leaves only 240 seconds for every backoff, probe, smoke check, floor, and setup, while the floor’s 50 subprocesses have no hard timeout (`.claude/scripts/profile-opus-4-7.py:67-83`). **Change:** choose either bounded automatic recovery or an explicit infrastructure-failure acceptance model, then pin exact backoffs, thresholds, probe caps, setup headroom, and an auditable worst-case inequality.
.claude/plans/PLAN-161/pair-rail/round-1.md:19:9. **P2 — C2 does not mechanically guarantee mode `0600`, and its report schema has nowhere for the promised hash.** Redirecting the redactor into a temporary file (`.claude/plans/PLAN-161-maintenance-sweep.md:251-265`) creates a mode determined by the caller’s umask—commonly `0644`. Separately, the current lane schema has no artifact-hash field (`.claude/workflows/council-audit.js:109-118`). **Change:** set `umask 077` or exclusively pre-create/chmod the file before rename, verify its mode before invoking Grok, and either add an attestation field through schema/reduction/report output or remove the hash-reporting claim.
.claude/plans/PLAN-161/pair-rail/round-1.md:25:12. **P2 — A document already touched by C2 contains another false council claim.** `.claude/commands/council.md:52-59` says missing arguments silently default the audit scope to `.`, but the workflow rejects missing or empty scope (`.claude/workflows/council-audit.js:61-68`); only fixture mode receives a fallback (`.claude/workflows/council-audit.js:70-71`). **Change:** correct this while amending the council command documentation.
